//@version=6
// ============================================================================
// SESSION TEST  v1.0
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ONE THING FIVE HYPOTHESES ALL IGNORED: THE CLOCK.
//
// Auction Cipher, the Trap Engine, the Positioning Engine, the magnet test and
// the regime test all derive a view from price and volume structure. Every one
// of them is blind to the time of day. A setup at 03:00 in the Asian lull and
// one at the New York open were counted as the same event.
//
// That is a different CATEGORY of claim, not another structural read, and it
// matters for three reasons:
//
//   1. Intraday seasonality is a DOCUMENTED market regularity. It is one of
//      the few effects that survives publication, because it comes from when
//      participants are awake and when instruments settle rather than from a
//      pattern anyone can arbitrage away by spotting it.
//
//   2. The drift confound that wrecked the magnet test handles itself here.
//      Hours are compared against the pooled hour baseline, so whatever the
//      sample's trend was, it is inside both sides of the comparison.
//
//   3. A clock result COMPOSES. If some hours are systematically different,
//      that is a filter that can be applied to anything else later, rather
//      than another standalone rule competing with the others.
//
// AND ONE CLAIM SITTING UNMEASURED IN YOUR OWN CODE.
//
// Auction Cipher computes `IB ext` and prints it on the dashboard. It has done
// so all along and nobody has ever checked whether it means anything. Auction
// theory says extending beyond the initial balance signals a trend day - that
// the auction has found one-timeframe direction and will keep going. That is
// a falsifiable claim about the rest of the session and this file tests it.
//
// HOW BOTH ARE RESOLVED: THE SAME SYMMETRIC RACE.
//
// From a start price, does +k ATR arrive before -k ATR? Equal distance both
// ways, so the arithmetic null is 50% and no stop, target, cost or R-multiple
// assumption enters the measurement. Ties inside one bar are discarded rather
// than guessed, and nothing is judged on a race's starting bar, whose high and
// low predate the close that registered it.
//
// This machinery is carried over unchanged from the regime test, where it
// worked correctly. What is new is only what triggers a race.
//
// WHAT WOULD COUNT.
//
// VOL is expected to vary by block. That is not a finding, it is a sanity
// check that the clock and the timezone are wired up correctly - if the
// volatility profile is flat, something is broken and nothing else on the
// panel should be believed.
//
// EDGE is the finding. An hour block whose up-rate departs from the pooled
// baseline by more than a few points, consistently across instruments, is
// directional seasonality. For the IB rows, a positive edge means extension
// continued - the claim auction theory actually makes.
//
// HONEST PRIOR, STATED BEFORE THE RUN: the volatility profile will almost
// certainly show structure, and that structure is real but not directly
// tradeable on a CFD account. Directional seasonality at this scale is much
// less likely and is the more competed effect. The IB claim is the one I would
// put weight on, because it is a specific mechanism and genuinely untested.
// ============================================================================
indicator("Session Test", shorttitle="SESSION", overlay=true,
     max_boxes_count=50, max_lines_count=100, max_labels_count=100, max_bars_back=500)

// ============================================================================
// 1. INPUTS
// ============================================================================
string G_S = "1 - SESSION"
string tzIn  = input.string("UTC", "Timezone", options=["UTC", "America/New_York", "Europe/London", "Asia/Tokyo"], group=G_S,
     tooltip="UTC by default so gold, silver and crypto are directly comparable. Switch to an exchange timezone only when studying one instrument's own session.")
int   ibMin  = input.int(60, "Initial Balance (minutes)", minval=15, maxval=480, step=15, group=G_S,
     tooltip="Classic IB is the first 60 minutes of a regular session - about a sixth of it. A 24-hour crypto day is four times longer, so 60 minutes is a twenty-fourth and price extends past it almost every day, which makes the test meaningless. Use 240 on 24-hour instruments to keep it the same SIXTH.\\n\\nThe panel prints the extension rate. If it is above about 90% the IB is too short for this instrument and the rows below mean nothing.")

string G_R = "2 - THE RACE"
float raceK    = input.float(1.0, "Race Distance (ATR)", minval=0.2, maxval=5.0, step=0.1, group=G_R,
     tooltip="Equal distance up and down is what makes the null exactly 50% and keeps every stop, target and cost assumption out of the measurement.")
int   raceBars = input.int(100, "Race Horizon (bars)", minval=10, maxval=500, group=G_R,
     tooltip="Races unresolved after this many bars are recorded as unresolved and excluded, rather than assigned to whichever side was closer.")

string G_D = "3 - DISPLAY"
bool showIB  = input.bool(true, "Draw Initial Balance", group=G_D)
bool showTbl = input.bool(true, "Results Table", group=G_D)

// ============================================================================
// 2. PRIMITIVES
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Top level, every bar, unconditional. Pine's ta.* functions carry rolling
// state and silently return stale values when reached through an `and` or a
// ternary - a bug that has already cost this project a whole round.
// ============================================================================
bool  barClosed = barstate.isconfirmed
float atr       = ta.atr(14)
int   chartSec  = timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period)

int   hr     = hour(time, tzIn)
int   blk    = hr / 3                       // eight three-hour blocks
bool  newDay = ta.change(time("D")) != 0
bool  newBlk = ta.change(blk) != 0

// ============================================================================
// 3. INITIAL BALANCE
// ============================================================================
var float ibH   = na
var float ibL   = na
var int   ibBar = 0
var bool  ibOn  = true
var bool  brkUp = false
var bool  brkDn = false

int ibBars = math.max(1, int(ibMin * 60 / math.max(chartSec, 1)))

if barClosed
    if newDay
        ibH   := high
        ibL   := low
        ibBar := 1
        ibOn  := true
        brkUp := false
        brkDn := false
    else if ibOn
        ibH := math.max(ibH, high)
        ibL := math.min(ibL, low)
        ibBar += 1
        if ibBar >= ibBars
            ibOn := false

// ============================================================================
// 4. RACES
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// bucket 0-7   the three-hour block the race started in
// bucket 8     first close ABOVE the initial balance high, once per session
// bucket 9     first close BELOW the initial balance low, once per session
//
// Block races are started one per block per session rather than one per bar,
// which keeps overlap - and therefore correlation between outcomes - far lower
// than a race on every bar would.
// ============================================================================
var array<float> rUp = array.new<float>()
var array<float> rDn = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   rBr = array.new<int>()
var array<int>   rBk = array.new<int>()

int NB = 10
var array<float> cN   = array.new<float>(NB, 0.0)
var array<float> cUp  = array.new<float>(NB, 0.0)
var array<float> cVol = array.new<float>(NB, 0.0)
var array<float> cVn  = array.new<float>(NB, 0.0)
var array<float> misc = array.new<float>(4, 0.0)   // 0 sessions  1 ext up  2 ext dn  3 unresolved

f_start(int bucket) =>
    bool ok = false
    if not na(atr) and atr > 0
        rUp.push(close + raceK * atr)
        rDn.push(close - raceK * atr)
        rBr.push(bar_index)
        rBk.push(bucket)
        ok := true
    ok

// volatility profile - a sanity check on the clock, not a finding
if barClosed and not na(atr) and atr > 0
    cVol.set(blk, cVol.get(blk) + (high - low) / atr)
    cVn.set(blk, cVn.get(blk) + 1)

if barClosed and newDay
    misc.set(0, misc.get(0) + 1)

if barClosed and newBlk
    f_start(blk)

if barClosed and not ibOn and not na(ibH)
    if not brkUp and close > ibH
        brkUp := true
        misc.set(1, misc.get(1) + 1)
        f_start(8)
    if not brkDn and close < ibL
        brkDn := true
        misc.set(2, misc.get(2) + 1)
        f_start(9)

f_race() =>
    if rUp.size() > 0
        for i = rUp.size() - 1 to 0
            int age = bar_index - rBr.get(i)
            int b   = rBk.get(i)
            int won = 0
            // Nothing is judged on the starting bar: it was registered at that
            // bar's close, but its high and low were made before that close
            // existed.
            if age >= 1
                bool up = high >= rUp.get(i)
                bool dn = low  <= rDn.get(i)
                // Both levels inside one bar is genuinely ambiguous, so it is
                // discarded. Guessing would quietly favour whichever side the
                // test hopes for.
                won := up and dn ? 0 : up ? 1 : dn ? -1 : 0
            if won != 0
                cN.set(b, cN.get(b) + 1)
                if won == 1
                    cUp.set(b, cUp.get(b) + 1)
                rUp.remove(i)
                rDn.remove(i)
                rBr.remove(i)
                rBk.remove(i)
            else if age >= raceBars
                misc.set(3, misc.get(3) + 1)
                rUp.remove(i)
                rDn.remove(i)
                rBr.remove(i)
                rBk.remove(i)
    rUp.size()

if barClosed
    f_race()

var line ibHL = na
var line ibLL = na
if showIB and barstate.islast and not na(ibH)
    line.delete(ibHL)
    line.delete(ibLL)
    ibHL := line.new(bar_index - ibBars, ibH, bar_index + 5, ibH, color=color.new(#787b86, 30))
    ibLL := line.new(bar_index - ibBars, ibL, bar_index + 5, ibL, color=color.new(#787b86, 30))

// ============================================================================
// 5. RESULTS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// BASE is every block race pooled - the drift this sample actually had. EDGE
// is each row against it, so a trending sample cannot make a block look
// directional when it is only riding the trend. That was the magnet test's
// confound and it is designed out here rather than discovered afterwards.
// ============================================================================
var table t = table.new(position.bottom_left, 5, 15, bgcolor=color.new(#131722, 10),
     border_color=color.new(#000000, 55), border_width=1)

f_cell(int c, int r, string v, color col, bool hdr) =>
    table.cell(t, c, r, v, text_color=col, text_size=size.tiny,
         text_halign=c == 0 ? text.align_left : text.align_right,
         bgcolor=hdr ? color.new(#2a2e39, 20) : color(na))

f_results() =>
    float bn = 0.0
    float bu = 0.0
    for b = 0 to 7
        bn += cN.get(b)
        bu += cUp.get(b)
    float base = bn > 0 ? bu / bn : na

    f_cell(0, 0, "BLOCK (" + tzIn + ")", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(1, 0, "N", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(2, 0, "UP%", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(3, 0, "EDGE", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(4, 0, "VOL", #d1d4dc, true)
    for b = 0 to 7
        float n = cN.get(b)
        float u = n > 0 ? cUp.get(b) / n : na
        float e = na(u) or na(base) ? na : (u - base) * 100
        color ec = n < 30 ? #b2b5be : na(e) ? #b2b5be : e > 5 ? #26a69a : e < -5 ? #ef5350 : #b2b5be
        f_cell(0, b + 1, str.tostring(b * 3, "00") + "-" + str.tostring(b * 3 + 3, "00"), #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(1, b + 1, str.tostring(int(n)), n < 30 ? #ff9800 : #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(2, b + 1, na(u) ? "-" : str.tostring(u * 100, "#.#") + "%", #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(3, b + 1, na(e) ? "-" : (e > 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(e, "#.#"), ec, false)
        f_cell(4, b + 1, cVn.get(b) > 0 ? str.tostring(cVol.get(b) / cVn.get(b), "#.##") : "-", #6c7080, false)

    f_cell(0, 9, "BASE  all blocks", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(1, 9, str.tostring(int(bn)), #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(2, 9, na(base) ? "-" : str.tostring(base * 100, "#.#") + "%", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(3, 9, "drift", #6c7080, true)
    f_cell(4, 9, "", #d1d4dc, true)

    // IB extension. For the DOWN row the call is that price keeps falling, so
    // its edge is measured against the down side of the baseline.
    float nu = cN.get(8)
    float uu = nu > 0 ? cUp.get(8) / nu : na
    float eu = na(uu) or na(base) ? na : (uu - base) * 100
    float nd = cN.get(9)
    float ud = nd > 0 ? cUp.get(9) / nd : na
    float ed = na(ud) or na(base) ? na : ((1 - ud) - (1 - base)) * 100
    f_cell(0, 10, "IB break UP -> continues", #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(1, 10, str.tostring(int(nu)), nu < 30 ? #ff9800 : #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(2, 10, na(uu) ? "-" : str.tostring(uu * 100, "#.#") + "%", #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(3, 10, na(eu) ? "-" : (eu > 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(eu, "#.#"),
         nu < 30 ? #b2b5be : na(eu) ? #b2b5be : eu > 5 ? #26a69a : eu < -5 ? #ef5350 : #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(4, 10, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(0, 11, "IB break DOWN -> continues", #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(1, 11, str.tostring(int(nd)), nd < 30 ? #ff9800 : #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(2, 11, na(ud) ? "-" : str.tostring((1 - ud) * 100, "#.#") + "%", #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(3, 11, na(ed) ? "-" : (ed > 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(ed, "#.#"),
         nd < 30 ? #b2b5be : na(ed) ? #b2b5be : ed > 5 ? #26a69a : ed < -5 ? #ef5350 : #b2b5be, false)
    f_cell(4, 11, "", #6c7080, false)

    // CALIBRATION. An IB that price leaves on almost every session is not a
    // balance, it is a formality, and the two rows above would be measuring
    // nothing. Same failure mode as the magnet test's classifier, labelled.
    float sess = misc.get(0)
    float extR = sess > 0 ? (misc.get(1) + misc.get(2)) / (sess * 2) * 100 : na
    f_cell(0, 12, "IB extension rate " + (na(extR) ? "-" : str.tostring(extR, "#.#") + "%") +
         (not na(extR) and extR > 90 ? "  IB TOO SHORT" : ""),
         na(extR) ? #6c7080 : extR > 90 ? #ef5350 : #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(1, 12, str.tostring(int(sess)) + " sess", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(2, 12, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(3, 12, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(4, 12, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(0, 13, "VOL is a sanity check, not a finding", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(1, 13, "unres " + str.tostring(int(misc.get(3))), #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(2, 13, "open " + str.tostring(rUp.size()), #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(3, 13, str.tostring(raceK, "#.#") + " ATR", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(4, 13, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(0, 14, "flat VOL profile = clock is broken", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(1, 14, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(2, 14, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(3, 14, "", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(4, 14, "", #6c7080, false)
    bn

if showTbl and barstate.islast
    f_results()
